 the same they should begin  striking agreements as to what they will  do with each other if there are  violations through their boundaries and  borders well I think I can agree with  all that but there are a few very tricky  problems let's say somebody in the US  writes something that is sort of Nazi  propaganda  which is clearly allowed by the freedom  of speech in the US and is clearly  banned by German laws who should deal  with that well the US government is not  I can almost assure you  gonna force a reading to be put on that  page in Germany you have many internet  service providers who none of whom have  the resources to filter the world net  and see if there's anything appropriate  out there so in between the person who  published in the US and the person who's  receiving that would say that Germans  don't want to have happen who's going to  find that and block it and unfortunately  in country by country you're gonna have  to get resources together to see which  things are out there that you don't want  to come in you can have a policy of only  letting things in that you review but  that would probably overwhelm you and  you'd lose the benefits in the internet  or have a policy of just blocking  certain name things but you'll have to  accept the fact that that things will  get through and so whatever cultural  restriction or whatever culture has  relied on restricting information flow  is it's not going to be that won't be  there as a tool as much as it as it has  been well I agree that there will be  imperfections I don't want to take more  time debating this let me make a couple  of announcements first of all following  immediately after this session will be  the president of South Africa mr.  Mandela who has agreed to come and speak  for a short while and the audience is  asked to please stay for his address the  second announcement I'm asked to make is  speakers please identify yourselves  your name and where you're from before  asking your question let's move on to a  next question please I have a question  Margaret Wilson chairman of Scarborough  is Austin Texas I'm interested in your  perspectives on future meetings of the  World Economic Forum either here in  Davos around the world and how  technology may affect these and how they  may be different as a result realizing  that Klaus Schwab has already brought a  great deal of technology here and  provided us with computers to use for  messages and other services and secondly  would be interested bill and you're  sharing a little bit more about with us  about your home as relating to  technology and how that  they affect our homes in the future okay  well if my home is the future then  there'll be a lot of large homes that  take a long time to get built the the  key neat thing I've done done with this  home is really pushed and limit on  screen technology and by put a lot of  screens all over the place that it'll be  quite some time before those are mass  market type items there are LCD  projector technologies there are there's  a chip technology called direct digital  mirror  device that's a texas instrument  approach and these are very neat ways of  displaying images with incredible  quality and so a company there are many  companies that are gathering image  libraries and putting those on to the  Internet I'm involved with a company  called Corvis that does that and does it  at very high resolution and those images  are indexed so that you can find them  easily so if I'm one day at home saying  I'm interested in Russia I can have  images come up on all the screens of the  house about Russia and if I'm passing by  one I'm curious about its background and  we're aiming just like that I just click  on a little remote control and get that  information and see more so whether it's  Renaissance art or sailboats or sunsets  any topic those images come in and it's  very fun informative and educational  that's probably the thing that that's  been done it's the most unusual okay let  me take the other part of your question  what about the World Economic Forum the  to answer that question it's a broader  question that you're raising I think  which is really what kind of activities  pass and do not pass what kind of human  activities pass and do not pass through  the wires and the wireless links of the  networked society and that's a  fascinating question let me suggest some  pretty simple things straight text  information images video pass rather  nicely video conferencing we could be  talking to each other and passing  signals to each other emotions I believe  they pass partially we cry and laugh in  front of our TV I don't see why we won't  cry and laugh in front of this do we  extend to cry and laugh here you can  carry that through the electronic world  but there are some things I submit that  do not pass at all and maybe I can  demonstrate can I touch you here good  thank you  if bill I don't know maybe he would  react badly yes if bill had not gone to  Harvard if he had come to MIT and if you  hadn't left Harvard god knows what will  happen to the software industry but it  would illustrate my point if he was  graduating from MIT and he was my  student and a good student and all that  I could grab him here by the collar and  I could say bill I expect you to do very  well when you graduate from here now I  could email that to him right but it  wouldn't be the same I could pass it  through video and it would be a little  better but still he wouldn't feel the  pain here or I could use the things we  described in our books which are the  tactile things you talked about and the  body nets and the body suits I talked  about so either he could wear a suit and  feel the pressure while he sees me  approaching or a robot could grab him I  mean any one of these solutions could  work but let me let me put a medical  show but let me make the point that that  is not the same thing now this is a  serious point why because both bill  knows and I know that we can turn off  the switch and stop the robot so the the  whole thing is cerebral it's not a  primal force and I believe these primal  forces of the cave the forces that we  felt when millions of years ago we were  in the cave the animal growling at the  door or you know the nurturing we felt  for a loved one or the food the wish for  those things those primal forces cannot  pass now you're going to say how many of  these primal forces  we use in the World Economic Forum I  suggest that we use a lot of them in our  daily life the relationships between  siblings between doctor and patients the  trust that we build with business  associates  are all based on the force of the case  and at MIT where were thinking of having  a virtual classes we will be requiring  students to come back every year or  every two semesters to take courses on  campus so they can feel these forces of  the cave build the trust recharge their  batteries so I think an answer to your  question a lot can happen through the  network but ultimately you'll have to  come back to divorce it's a it's a very  good question because although face to  face meetings will always be superior in  many many respects to even the best  video conferencing environments  sometimes a face to face meeting is so  much trouble you'll take the the lesser  approach videoconferencing business to  business is going to catch on wildly I  mean you know when people see this  welcome system a lot of what they're  responding to is high quality  videoconferencing whose price is getting  to be very reasonable and so we need you  know it's it's a very good question how  many things like this they'll be the  speeches we're all sitting in a room  here that can be recreated through  technology very easily what can't be  recreated is when you get up and you're  sort of mingling around meeting somebody  new running into somebody at dinner and  kind of the isolation we have that we're  all here in in this common meeting which  is a very important element of it okay I  think we've answered this one enough  let's go to another question yes and  then on the other side please  first here my question is my name is IEC  Makani president of Afghan line my  question would be given that the  internet will encourage forces of  capitalism isn't there going to be a  time when we're going to need regulation  and if  we're gonna need regulation who would be  doing it otherwise the whole system will  crack at some stage well I think I  answered this by asking for regulation  first within the countries for the  activities within the country and then  pairwise regulations as we do with trade  and crime for cross-border activities  but bill but you may want to add more to  that the the question was about  restrictions on content primarily no  it's mostly international mostly from an  international point of view you're gonna  get certain companies getting really big  the extent probably where they can  control regulation and other governments  and wouldn't there come a stage when we  probably should start regulating it  early enough before it's too late  is there a need for that and if there is  who would be the right body at the  international scale regulation at this  stage well it's never too late this is  probably targeted towards Bill and  Microsoft because there are people  already complain it it's already too big  well geez Microsoft's actually pretty  small company  honestly it may be one of the smallest  companies here at this event it's  certainly not in the top thousand large  companies if you know sectors of the  economy where prices can come down by a  factor of four as they have in software  by having such rapid improvements those  are those are the ultra competitive  sectors of the economy and I wish I was  in an industry where somebody's future  was guaranteed but if you take any  industry and you say what leader of an  industry might be to placed in the next  10 years you would pick the leaders of  the technology business they are the  most at risk they are sort of surfing  the wave if they miss a technology if  they don't list them all to their  customers they don't hire smart people  they are gone whereas in many other  businesses you have brand assets  manufacturing assets distribution assets  that are great protection and that's why  the technology business people  they run scared certainly we do in terms  of thinking boy will we be the one to do  new products so I think the the toughest  parts of this relate to the network in  pricing of the network because there  it's it's more economical to only have  one person build the lot of the very  high speed elements of the network and  yet the having the right touch of  regulation where you and you encourage  innovation and yet you know what the  pricing model should be that still has  yet to be figured out I do not have this  fear of bigness corporate bigness  because the technology helps the  individual as much or more than it helps  the large company let me explain in the  industrial age you could not build a car  or any other major product in your  living room or in your study but in this  information age you can certainly sell  your information work or your  information products or services from  your home so I think what this does it  gives a capability to a lot of  individual people to sell products  services information as well as to  freely exchange it and I think it  counteracts some of the fears that you  have about about signs the other thing  it does and it was raised here in this  in this forum one or two years ahead by  Lord  Yehudi Menuhin and it was the capability  of this new technology to make the  millions of voiceless people up to now  become heard so I think we have some  positive things ahead of us to look  forward to let's have another question  mr. gates Alexei Pushkov Russian Public  Television you were pretty critical of  people who are spending hours before a  television screen but there were some  reports in the press that your company  together with NBC is investing few  billion dollars into a global television  broadcasting system is it is it the  right information first if it is how  will it affect the nature of the  television and what is the different  difference from the today's satellite  testing television no there's no meaning  in MBC together with Microsoft has done  a news service and an internet site but  certainly there's no investments of  billions of dollars or anything related  to a satellite infrastructure there it's  just one of the many news services that  you'll find on the internet and there's  some very very healthy competition there  ladies and gentlemen we have some new  high-level guests with us and we have  been asked to finish this session to  close it now so that we may hear from a  high-level guest so I would like to  thank Bill and the audience for a very  exciting and informative session thank  you  you

